{"question":"Why do the observer-run instruments remain offset from the lab-run instruments by a stable amount, while each stream stays internally stable and cross-stream similarity does not survive offset/lkag searches beyond chance?","hypotheses":[{"label":"Observer-side calibration/zeroing bias in the measurement workflow","probability":0.52,"rationale":"This still best fits the full pattern: both observer instruments are shifted relative to lab instruments, yet each stream is internally stable and shows no convincing synchronized level change. That is exactly what a consistent setup, zeroing, handling, or calibration offset would produce. The new Day 16 values continue the same separation without any sign of a common step or convergence.","evidenceFor":[920324,341617,91746,124418,28005,939295,1011131,343676,346059,436587,252848,866707,226664,1005277,56331,10105,594153,10138,31470,665280,543372,867571,170772,999803,221646],"evidenceAgainst":[]
},{"label":"The observer and lab setups are effectively measuring different scales or related but non-identical quantities","probability":0.19,"rationale":"A stable offset can arise even without a simple calibration mistake if the two setups encode slightly different transfer functions, reference standards, or operating regimes. This remains plausible because the separation appears in both pendulum and resonator families, which suggests a systematic difference in the measurement chain rather than an isolated fault. I keep it below the calibration-bias explanation because the pattern still looks more like a procedural offset than a principled redefinition of the measured quantity.","evidenceFor":[920324,343676,346059,436587,252848,866707,226664,665280,543372,867571,170772,999803,221646],"evidenceAgainst":[]
},{"label":"A weak environment or shared mechanical influence is affecting the observer instruments more than the lab instruments","probability":0.08,"rationale":"This remains possible, but the records do not show a clean common-mode signature beyond the stable offset itself. If an external factor were responsible, I would expect more obvious linkage across observer instruments or a clearer temporal structure. The current evidence is compatible with a mild environmental/mechanical contribution, but it is not the leading explanation.","evidenceFor":[920324,665280,543372,867571,170772,999803,221646],"evidenceAgainst":[343676,346059,436587,252848,866707,226664,1005277,56331,10105,594153,10138,31470]
},{"label":"The offset is mostly sampling noise amplified by searching across many lags/offsets","probability":0.1,"rationale":"Multiple-comparison effects do weaken the apparent strength of the cross-checks: the best matches are only marginally different from chance once the offset search is accounted for. That said, pure noise does not easily explain the repeated within-stream stability and the consistent observer-versus-lab separation across both instrument types. So chance contributes, but cannot carry the whole explanation.","evidenceFor":[920324,343676,346059,436587,252848,866707,226664,1005277,56331,10105,594153,10138,31470,665280,543372,867571,170772,999803,221646],"evidenceAgainst":[]
},{"label":"Some other specific instrumental fault, protocol difference, or unlogged interaction is responsible","probability":0.07,"rationale":"This is the best catch-all for a mundane but not-yet-isolated cause. The pattern strongly implies a systematic issue on the observer side, but the available records do not identify which mechanism. This bucket stays available because the evidence supports a reproducible process difference more strongly than any single named failure mode.","evidenceFor":[920324,343676,346059,436587,252848,866707,226664,1005277,56331,10105,594153,10138,31470,665280,543372,867571,170772,999803,221646],"evidenceAgainst":[]
}],"residual":0.04,"summaryOfChange":"I kept the same core anomaly because today's Day 16 data continue the established pattern: internally stable streams, persistent lab/observer separation, and no new synchronized level change. I slightly increased the weight on explicit calibration/protocol bias and reduced the residual, while keeping chance and environmental explanations secondary because the evidence still looks systematic rather than random or externally driven."}
